From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 23:37:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Populate an index created on an object store with
 records

createIndex on a store that has records gives the new index the records
of the store's records: index creation is "processed as an asynchronous
request within the upgrade transaction", and records that would give a
unique index a key twice abort the upgrade transaction with a
ConstraintError (https://w3c.github.io/IndexedDB/#dom-idbobjectstore-createindex).
Before this, the index stayed empty for the existing records. Script
drives the population, as in Chromium (design decision 7):

- CreateIndex carries `populate`, unless the upgrade created the store
  and made no put or add on it. The backend creates the index and
  reports the store's first records (IndexPopulationEvent::Records), at
  most 500 or up to 1 MiB of values.
- For each batch, script asks for the next one (PopulateIndex
  ReadRecords) before it deserializes the values, with the generated-key
  injection of reads, and computes their index keys with the code of
  puts; it sends them as SetIndexKeys, the last batch's with `done`, and
  the backend answers Done. As for puts, the transaction is inactive
  while the keys are computed, so requests from page code that this
  reaches throw a TransactionInactiveError; if that code aborts the
  transaction, script computes no more keys and sends nothing.
- The database actor keeps the transaction's requests and schema
  operations that arrive after the CreateIndex waiting, in order, while
  only the population's steps run, each as it arrives (engine.rs
  Population, Chromium's preemptive queue): requests made after
  createIndex see the whole index, and a later deleteIndex,
  deleteObjectStore or createIndex runs after it, so a second population
  sees the records added before its createIndex.
  A unique index that gets a key twice fails the transaction with the new
  BackendError::Constraint. A transaction that failed during a
  population drops it when it commits, as when it aborts.
- In script the population is a request list entry without an
  IDBRequest, so it ends in request order: the abort with ConstraintError
  comes after the events of the requests made before createIndex, and the
  transaction can't commit before the population ends. An index or store
  deleted meanwhile is still populated, since the requests made between
  see the index.

A transaction that the backend, or script on its behalf, fails aborts
with an "UnknownError" DOMException, as step 2.4 of "commit a
transaction" suggests, with the neutral message "The IndexedDB
transaction failed" and the details only in the log
(map_transaction_error_to_dom_error): a stored value that doesn't
deserialize while populating, and a population, schema operation or
commit that fails in the backend. A unique index's conflict stays a
ConstraintError and a full disk a QuotaExceededError. Servo's Error and
DOMException get UnknownError for this; before, such failures were
OperationErrors, a schema operation's with "IndexedDB open failed" and
the backend's details. Opening and deleting a database keep their
mapping.

Validation: `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` (host SQLite): 133 passed, 1 skipped (P3.1 122/1), with population tests on every executor (index records equal to those of puts over three batches with multiEntry, unique and keyless records; unique conflicts across batches, inside a batch and in a later check statement; requests and a deleteIndex that wait for the population; populations in turn; abort during a population; an empty store; the batch size limit) and an integration test through the manager. Host WPT tests/wpt/tests/IndexedDB, 8 processes, with the next commit (which adds only a connection queue fix): SpiderMonkey 3,392, QuickJS 3,389 of 4,511 (P3.1 3,369 / 3,371); the new passes on both engines are idbobjectstore_createIndex's four population subtests, idbtransaction_abort and idbfactory-open-request-error, each in window, worker and sharedworker (18), the other differences known worker/sharedworker whole-test flips; expectations updated with `./mach update-wpt` from both engines' runs. At this commit those three tests and the new tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/index-population.any.js (9 subtests in 4 globals) run as expected on SM; the fork test also passes on QuickJS and with --multiprocess. A page probe stores the same records in two databases, with the indexes created before and after the puts, and reads identical index records back from both SQLite files, generated keys included, on SM and QuickJS. DAYU200 (QuickJS + RDB) with the next commit: WPT IndexedDB/ 3,381 (P3.1 3,367) with the same 18 new passes, /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 172 of 172, `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage` RDB only 135 passed / 3 skipped, SQLite and RDB 239 / 4. Populating 10,000 records takes 1.55 s on the board (4.7 s with a statement per key) and 13 ms on the host, linear up to 20,000; RSS rises by the same ~70 MiB for 200 MB and 600 MB stores. `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings`, `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --use-crown --locked --profile checked-release` and `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` with SQLite, RDB and both pass. With the P3 review fixes folded in: `./mach check` without warnings, test-tidy without new errors (the UnknownError arm raises error.rs's allowance to 30) and nextest 134 passed, 1 skipped at this commit, with the new test_commit_of_a_transaction_that_failed_while_populating, which fails a debug assertion at the next upgrade's population without the fix; with the new early return of run_waiting broken, 11 population tests (backend and integration) fail. index-population.any.js gets 2 subtests (11 in 4 globals: a getter that the population reaches and that throws, whose subkey is skipped and whose requests throw, or that aborts the upgrade, which leaves the database at its old version), which pass at the branch tip on SM, QuickJS and the DAYU200; the P3.3 binary fails the aborting one in all 4 globals, as it computed the keys of the whole batch after the abort.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; designed and implemented the population protocol, the backend hold and the script driver, wrote the unit, integration and WPT tests, ran the host, board and performance checks; after the P3 review, computed the keys with the transaction inactive, ran population steps as they arrive, fixed the commit of a failed population, added UnknownError and wrote the tests)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
